def main(inp):
    import openpyxl
    from openpyxl.utils import get_column_letter
    wb1 = openpyxl.load_workbook('input.xlsx')
    wb2 = openpyxl.load_workbook('output.xlsx')
    diffs = []
    for sn in wb2.sheetnames:
        if sn not in wb1.sheetnames:
            continue
        s1, s2 = wb1[sn], wb2[sn]
        mr = max(s1.max_row or 0, s2.max_row or 0)
        mc = max(s1.max_column or 0, s2.max_column or 0)
        for r in range(1, mr + 1):
            for c in range(1, mc + 1):
                v1 = s1.cell(r, c).value
                v2 = s2.cell(r, c).value
                if v1 != v2:
                    addr = f"{get_column_letter(c)}{r}"
                    diffs.append(f"{sn}!{addr}: {repr(v1)} -> {repr(v2)}")
    report = f"CELL DIFF REPORT: {len(diffs)} cells changed\n"
    for d in diffs[:300]:
        report += f"- {d}\n"
    if len(diffs) > 300:
        report += f"({len(diffs)-300} more not listed)\n"
    if not diffs:
        report = "CELL DIFF REPORT: No cells changed.\n"
    return {'facts': {'cell_diff': report}, 'files': {}}

sections.operator_lines:
The structural operators, each one line: what kind of step (`agent`, `op` or `code`), where (a step id), and its text (a prompt, or code). The system does the wiring, as each operator's line below says. Ids, files and facts are generated; each text goes into a knob of its own (`<id>__task`, `<id>__code`) for a later `set_knob`; the candidate says what was wired. Every text has a default (the neighbour's, or a neutral line), and each operator counts as one change. The written-out operations in the table above stay for what these do not express.
- `insert` (after, before, kind, text, system, sees, to, id, call, reads, tools, takes_over, sealed, produces, writes): a new step after (or before) a step; 'before' puts it on every edge into that step, so 'sees' must name only what every path carries there: 'kind' agent (text = its prompt; 'system' its system prompt), code (text = Python) or op (with 'call' and 'reads'); it sees what that step sees plus its outputs; its output goes into the nearest prompt after it that can take it -- an agent's own prompt knob, or the one text knob an op fills facts into -- or where 'to' says (a prompt knob, or a step: an agent whose prompt is no knob gets a section for it); with neither, refused unless a step reads it; 'tools' (an agent): e.g. ["bash"]; a new step may have them though the steps beside it have none or refuse theirs (an existing step keeps its own tool rules); it runs sealed: only the files it sees (in the per-item part, without 'sees', the item's input files too), no network, no grader files; 'sealed' true on a code step: its code runs in the Task's tool environment (its Python interpreter, its extra commands) sealed -- the files it sees read-only, no network, no grader files -- so it may import any package the Task provides (openpyxl, pandas, numpy) and read item files; the import allowlist does not apply; the Task must declare a tool environment; 'takes_over' an earlier step: the new step writes that step's outputs, so what read them reads the new step's (a choice among attempts reaching what is scored), and that step's own go under new names; 'takes_over' as [step, output, ...] takes only those outputs (the new step writes them and its own; the rest pass through unchanged, so downstream steps keep reading them from the original step). E.g. {"op": "insert", "after": "implement", "kind": "agent", "text": "<what the new step does>"}
- `remove` (step, keep): take a step out and reconnect around it (a fork branch too); on a gate, 'keep' names the verdict whose path stays. E.g. {"op": "remove", "step": "<step>"}
- `parallelize` (steps, copies, after, new, sees): run consecutive 'steps' at once, or one step beside 'copies' of itself, or 'new' steps after 'after', between a fork and a join. E.g. {"op": "parallelize", "steps": ["implement"], "copies": 2}
- `serialize` (fork): run a fork's branches one after another, in their order. E.g. {"op": "serialize", "fork": "<fork>"}
- `branch` (fork, kind, text, system, sees, optional, id, to, tools, sealed, produces, writes): a new step as another branch of a fork ('optional': true lets it fail without failing the item; 'sees' names its inputs: "task", step ids, or single fact or file names there before the fork; 'system' as insert's); its output goes into the nearest prompt after the join that can take it -- an agent's own prompt knob, or the one text knob an op fills facts into -- or where 'to' says (a prompt knob, or a step: an agent whose prompt is no knob gets a section for it); with neither, refused unless a step reads it; 'tools' as insert's (sealed); 'sealed' as insert's on a code step. E.g. {"op": "branch", "fork": "<fork>", "kind": "agent", "text": "<what this branch does>"}
- `rewire` (step, sees, text): change what a step sees: "task" (the task's own input, once, as the model steps receive it: never a step's output or a knob's text (an op's output carries the knobs the op declares for it in `knob_outputs`, else every knob the op reads)), step ids (their outputs), or single fact or file names there before it (that input alone); 'text' gives an agent step new instructions too, in place: the same id, outputs and place in a fork or join (so a branch can work from the task alone); with 'text' and no 'sees' it keeps what it sees. E.g. {"op": "rewire", "step": "implement", "sees": ["task"]}
- `loop` (step, back_to, judge, text, sees, tools, max_iterations, id, sealed): a judge after a step that can send it back with its reason -- or, with 'back_to', send the work back to an earlier agent step, so every step from there to the judge runs again (a fork in between whole); 'back_to' is one every run passes before the step, in the same per-item pass and fork branch: 'judge' agent (text = what to check; its answer starts with proceed or reloop, then why), code (text = Python returning {'verdict': 'proceed' or 'reloop', 'reason': why}) or a callable. The judge is handed what the step read and wrote, unless 'sees' says otherwise, as text (a binary or very long file only named); with 'tools' (an agent judge, as insert's) it runs sealed instead, with the files it sees in its workspace, and its reply starts with the verdict; with 'sealed' true on a code judge, it runs in the Task's tool environment sealed, as insert's 'sealed' on a code step. The reason reaches the step sent back, with that step's own last output; it is sent back at most 'max_iterations' times, so it runs at most that many times plus one (default 2: up to 3 runs; 1 to 5; afterwards the knob <gate>__max_iterations, the gate being <step>_check unless 'id' names it), then the run goes on. E.g. {"op": "loop", "step": "implement", "judge": "agent", "text": "<what to check>", "max_iterations": 2}
- `route` (after, judge, text, to, sees, tools, id, sealed): a judge after a step chooses one labelled next step ('to': label -> step id or a new step, whose 'takes_over' rewrites an earlier step's outputs on that way); the branches rejoin; with no 'to', proceed or redo the step in place; 'tools' as loop's judge. E.g. {"op": "route", "after": "implement", "judge": "agent", "text": "<how to choose>", "to": {"proceed": "verify", "revise": {"kind": "agent", "text": "<what it does>"}}}
- `split` (step, into: one or two steps, each like {kind, text, sees}): replace a step by two new steps run in turn ('into' two), or by one ('into' one: replace); the last takes over its outputs. E.g. {"op": "split", "step": "<step>", "into": [{"kind": "agent", "text": "<first part>"}, {"kind": "agent", "text": "<second part>"}]}
- `merge` (steps, kind, text, system, id, tools, sealed): two consecutive steps as one, with the first's inputs and the second's outputs; 'system' as insert's; 'tools' as insert's (sealed), else a sealed step's own come along; 'sealed' as insert's on a code step. E.g. {"op": "merge", "steps": ["<step>", "<next step>"], "text": "<what the merged step does>"}
- `fallback` (step, kind, text, system, sees, id, call, reads, tools, sealed): a step run instead when 'step' (one run per item) fails -- it raises, crashes past its retries, hits its output limit, its turn cap, its prompt limit, or is still writing at the session time limit (backstop); never for a timeout (the harness's, a declared wall or the step's own) or a harness fault: it writes that step's outputs, so what reads them still gets an answer; 'kind' as in insert; it sees what 'step' sees; when the step writes more than one file, the fallback must be a code step or an agent with tools (a plain agent writes only its reply); 'sealed' as insert's on a code step. E.g. {"op": "fallback", "step": "<step>", "kind": "agent", "text": "<a simpler way to the same output>"}
- `map` (after, over, text, system, sees, max, combine, id, to, tools): after a step, a new agent step run once per element of 'over' (a list there: a fact, a file or a step's output), one model call each, all at once: a JSON array gives its items, a bulleted, numbered or lettered list its items, other text its non-empty lines; at most 'max' run (default 4, up to 8; past it the first 'max', and the combine is told so); 'text' says what to do with one element; 'combine' (a step as in insert) sees every result and writes one output, placed as insert's is; 'system' as insert's, for each run; 'tools' as insert's: each run sealed; remove the elements step or the combine and the whole map goes. E.g. {"op": "map", "after": "implement", "over": "implement", "text": "<what to do with one element>", "combine": {"kind": "agent", "text": "<how to combine the results>"}}
- in the per-item part `data`, a step with `tools` runs its commands with python3 (and python) = Python 3.12 with openpyxl 3.1.5, pandas 2.3.3, numpy 2.5.4; view_xlsx (SpreadsheetBench 2's workbook viewer); soffice and libreoffice (LibreOffice, headless); a writable /tmp of the step's own
